          "text": "# Spain: consistent, co-ordinated protection for women facing intersecting discrimination\n\n## Policy objective\n\nSpain should translate its extensive policy commitments into consistent protection, support and access to justice across all territories. Article 7 of the Istanbul Convention requires comprehensive, co-ordinated measures centred on victims’ rights, addressing the specific circumstances and barriers experienced by women facing multiple and intersecting forms of discrimination.\n\n## Main findings\n\nGREVIO welcomes Spain’s expanded policy framework: the State Strategy to Combat Male Violence 2022-2025 covers all convention forms of violence, while the Multi-Annual Joint Plan 2023-2027 establishes national–regional co-operation and a reference catalogue of minimum services. Intersectionality is recognised in national policies, including strategies concerning Roma people, disability and homelessness. These advances provide a foundation, but implementation remains uneven.\n\nWomen facing intersecting discrimination still encounter substantial barriers to protection and support. Migrant women, women with disabilities and rural women are disproportionately represented among victims. Professionals often address only one discrimination factor, overlooking overlapping barriers, particularly for migrant women, women in prostitution, LBTI women and women with disabilities.\n\nCo-ordination remains weak between administrative levels and autonomous communities. Unclear implementation priorities contribute to unequal application of the State Pact; the 19 Co-ordination Units need further resources. Evaluation remains insufficiently systematic, thorough and independent. Previously missing indicators and assigned responsibilities impeded scrutiny. NGOs, particularly those supporting women facing intersecting discrimination, lack institutionalised channels for regular participation.\n\n## Priorities for policymakers\n\n- **Make intersectionality operational:** ensure existing policies meet victims’ specific needs and that individual responses recognise multiple, interacting forms of discrimination rather than treating each factor separately. Keep victims’ rights central to protection, support and justice.\n\n- **Strengthen territorial consistency:** clarify State Pact implementation priorities, use the Joint Plan and minimum-service catalogue to support co-ordinated delivery, and provide the Co-ordination Units with further resources to perform their role effectively across autonomous communities.\n\n- **Evaluate implementation regularly:** build on the strategy’s indicators and evaluation system and the Ministry of Equality’s computerised monitoring system. Use predefined indicators, clear stakeholder responsibilities and systematic, thorough, independent evaluation to assess impact, identify implementation difficulties and underpin policy making with reliable data.\n\n- **Institutionalise NGO participation:** establish regular channels for organisations working with women victims, especially those exposed to intersecting discrimination, to contribute to policy making, monitoring and evaluation. Their direct experience should inform assessments of whether existing measures reach women across populations and regions, helping authorities identify barriers and remedy gaps between policy commitments and the protection and support delivered in practice.",
